### One Signature Certificates
- **Presenter**: Stefan Santesson
- **Slides**: [One Signature Certificates](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-one-signature-certificates-00)
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-lamps-one-signature-certs`
- **Discussion**:
  - Stefan Santesson outlined updates to the specification for short-lived certificates where keys are generated for a single document signature and immediately destroyed. 
  - Key Usage has been constrained to `digitalSignature` and `nonRepudiation` (plus key usage required for certificate request proof-of-possession). Binding type identifiers were converted from text strings to OIDs.
  - Deb Cooley asked if key deletion is technically enforced or procedural. Stefan Santesson and Russ Housley clarified that while deletion relies on CA/RA operational assertions, the certificate explicitly includes an extension binding the certificate to the hash of the specific signed document, preventing any key reuse even if a private key were compromised or retained.
  - Victor Kuarskey suggested recommending ordinary document signing Extended Key Usage (EKU) for compatibility with standard verifiers.
- **Next Steps**: Add introductory text clarifying document signing EKU applicability, finalize OID assignments, and issue WG Last Call.

