### S/MIMEv5 Updates
- **Presenter**: Sean Turner
- **Slides**: [S/MIMEv5](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-smimev5-00)
- **Drafts**: `draft-ietf-lamps-rfc8550bis`, `draft-ietf-lamps-rfc8551bis`
- **Discussion**:
  - **Structure & Modules**: Obsoleting v4; updating ASN.1 modules to 2021 standards; removing obsolete algorithms (e.g., RC2) from S/MIME capability sets.
  - **Encrypted & Signed Data**: Proposed dropping EnvelopedData and CBC modes entirely, mandating AuthenticatedData / AuthEnvelopedData. Standardizing SignedData to mandate three core signed attributes and require checking recipient types.
  - **Certificate Handling**: Adding requirements for clients to handle common certificate extensions (Certificate Policies, CRL Distribution Points) and reject hazardous configurations (e.g., `anyExtendedKeyUsage` in end-entity certs).
  - **Obsolete Cleanup**: Dropping support for v1 certificates/CRLs, SHA-1 signing cert attributes, and legacy 8-character filename restrictions.
  - **Unintrusive Signatures**: Daniel Kahn Gillmor presented the work ongoing in MAILMAINT/OpenPGP on unintrusive signatures (which includes header protection via RFC 9788). Sean Turner proposed referencing this as an alternative to legacy `multipart/signed`.
  - **Compression**: Sean Turner questioned whether to update Zlib or drop compressed data entirely. Rich Salz, Daniel Kahn Gillmor, and Deb Cooley supported dropping compression from S/MIME to align with TLS, SSH, and IPsec deprecations and avoid compression side-channel risks.
  - **Algorithm Selection & MTI**: Extensive discussion on mandatory-to-implement (MTI) algorithms for PQC and hybrid schemes. Participants (John Bradley, Daniel Kahn Gillmor, Deb Cooley, Hannes Tschofenig) strongly favored paring down algorithm choices, dropping P-521 and legacy RSA from MTI, and defining a tight set of MTI algorithms specific to S/MIME rather than trying to support all algorithm combinations.

### Algorithm Identifiers for FrodoKEM in X.509
- **Presenter**: Valery Smyslov
- **Slides**: [X.509 - Algorithm Identifiers for FrodoKEM](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-x509-algorithm-identifiers-for-frodokem-00)
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-lamps-frodokem-certificates`
- **Discussion**:
  - Valery Smyslov raised the question of whether to retain `eFrodoKEM` (ephemeral parameter set with a hard key reuse limit of 256 uses) in X.509 certificates.
  - Corey Bonnell, Scott Fluhrer, Thom Wiggers (in chat), and others strongly advocated for removing `eFrodoKEM`, stating that a 256-signature reuse limit makes no sense for certificates and the ~48–64 byte size savings are negligible compared to total public key sizes (9–13 KB).
- **Next Steps**: Confirm removal of `eFrodoKEM` on the mailing list.

### Use of FrodoKEM in CMS
- **Presenter**: Milling Zhang
- **Slides**: [Use of FrodoKEM in the Cryptographic Message Syntax](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-use-of-frodokem-in-the-cryptographic-message-syntax-00)
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-lamps-cms-frodokem`
- **Discussion**:
  - The document has been adopted as a working group draft. Added ASN.1 modules, IANA considerations, updated references to the ISO FrodoKEM standard, and cleaned up redundant identifiers.
  - Michael StJohns noted a discrepancy in private key representation between the ISO standard and the CFRG draft that must be resolved. Scott Fluhrer recommended dropping `eFrodoKEM` and selecting either AES or SHAKE variants rather than keeping both.

### RFC 3161 Update (Time-Stamp Protocol)
- **Presenter**: Russ Housley
- **Slides**: [RFC 3161 Update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-rfc-3161-update-00)
- **Discussion**:
  - Addresses a cross-protocol attack between RFC 3161 timestamps and Microsoft Authenticode timestamps signed with the same key.
  - Proposes updating RFC 3161 to:
    1. Add security considerations detailing the cross-protocol attack.
    2. Mandate that applications MUST reject a timestamp token if the `id-kp-timeStamping` extended key usage is missing from the TSA certificate.
    3. Mandate that applications MUST reject a timestamp token if the content type is missing.
- **Next Steps**: Complete the ongoing call for adoption and proceed directly to Working Group Last Call.

### Certificate Authentication Context Type for OpenID Connect
- **Presenter**: Stefan Santesson
- **Slides**: [Certificate Authentication Context Type for OpenID Connect](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-certificate-authentication-context-type-for-openid-connect-00)
- **Discussion**:
  - Defines an OpenID Connect (OIDC) authentication context type extension for RFC 7773 to bridge identity claims from OIDC identity providers into short-lived/on-the-fly signature certificates.
  - Specifies a JSON structure in `contextInfo` containing provider issuer, authentication time, ACR level, transaction ID, client ID, and claim-to-RDN mappings.
  - Requests an IANA registry for context types and an IETF URN. Relaxes criticality rules in RFC 7773.
- **Next Steps**: Russ Housley asked for objections to issuing a call for adoption; none were raised.

### Composite FrodoKEM for X.509 and CMS
- **Presenter**: Yubing Chen
- **Slides**: [Composite FrodoKEM for X.509 and CMS](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-composite-frodokem-for-x509-and-cms-00)
- **Discussion**:
  - Proposed specifying composite FrodoKEM combinations with traditional algorithms (RSA-OAEP, ECDH P-256/P-384, X25519, X448) using the composite KEM framework.
  - Panos Kampanakis, Vishmeet Sikka, and Scott Fluhrer questioned the security rationale, size overhead (15–26 KB), and deployment demand. Mike Ounsworth suggested merging hybrid/composite FrodoKEM parameters directly into `draft-ietf-lamps-frodokem-certificates` rather than maintaining separate drafts.

### Embedded Alternate Issuer Certificates ("Turducken / PQ Opt-Out Certs")
- **Presenter**: David Benjamin
- **Slides**: [Embedded Alternate Issuer Certificates](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lamps-embedded-alternate-issuer-certificates-00)
- **Discussion**:
  - Addresses WebPKI migration to Post-Quantum CAs when legacy servers cannot be updated to support PQC certs or multi-cert negotiation. 
  - Embeds an alternate PQ-rooted certificate ("opt-out certificate") inside a legacy certificate extension/SCT structure. Legacy clients validate the outer legacy cert while updated PQ clients extract and validate the embedded PQ cert.
  - John Gray and Carl Wallace confirmed successful interoperability during the IETF 127 Hackathon.
  - Sophie Schmieg and David Benjamin discussed certificate size bloat and backward compatibility risks.
- **Next Steps**: Authors will request WG adoption on the mailing list.

